## Introduction
Biological ion channels embedded within cell membranes play vital roles in life processes[1]. Inspired by nature, the artificial nanochannels have attracted enormous research interest owing to their outstanding tunability for desired functionalities, providing wide applications in nanofluidic devices,[2] biosensors,[3] energy conversion[4] and ion separation.[5] Gating function is a basic feature for ion channels, which means that the ion conduction through the channel can be regulated between an open state (with high ion flux) and a close state (with low or without ion flux) by external stimuli. The artificial molecular gates of biomimetic ion channels are usually constructed by functionalizing the inner surface of one-dimensional (1D) nanochannels with various responsive molecules or responsive systems[6]. Rapid progress has been made in the development of artificial ion channels with smart gating functions, which open and close in response to a series of different stimuli, including single stimulus, such as pH,[7]
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs)[15] are a class of porous materials composed of metal ions or clusters as centers and organic ligands as linkers. Attributed to their highly tailorable framework structures, uniform pore sizes, and high porosity, MOFs are of great interest for applications in gas separation and storage,[16] catalysis,[17] sensor technology,[18] and drug delivery[19]. Particularly, the development of water-stable MOFs[20] enables their new applications in water processing, nanofluidic devices, and ion separation.[21] For example, a water-stable, polycrystalline UiO-66 (Zr) MOF membrane was fabricated on alumina hollow fibers for water softening application.[22] A crack-free subnanometer composite structure for the study of ion transport was constructed by in-situ growth of ZIF-90 [Zn(Imidazole-2- carboxaldehyde)2] on the tip of a glass nanopipette.[23] Our group recently reported ultrafast and selective alkali metal ion transport in a high-quality ZIF-8 [Zn(2-methylimidazolate)2] membrane with sub-nanometer pores.[24] Very recently, our group further developed highly fluoride ion conductive and selective subnanofluidic UiO-66-X $x=H$ , $N H_{2}$ , ${N}^{+}(\mathsf{C}\mathsf{H}_{3})_{3}$ channels fabricated by growing MOFs into polymeric nanochannels.[5b] Although these works have demonstrated the feasibility of using water-stable MOF materials to construct artificial ion channels with pore sizes down to angstrom scale, no study of artificial angstrom channels with switchable ion transport properties has been reported yet. Previous studies of smart MOFs functionalized with responsive molecules for switchable gas adsorption provided possibility for constructing switchable ion channel. AZO is one of the most popular photo-responsive organic groups, as AZO group can undergo reversible isomerization between trans and cis when triggered by UV irradiation or heat[25]. Taking AZO-containing photochromic MOFs as an example, these MOFs can be synthesized by incorporating the responsive groups into the linkers of the framework[26] or by inserting the smart guest molecules into the MOF cavities, where MOFs serve as host materials[27]. When AZO is introduced into the MOF pores, its transformation upon stimuli would change the effective size of the MOF channel for ion conduction. On the other hand, with dimensional compatibility to MOF window size, commercially available simple AZO could be applied as molecule gate to efficiently control the ion transportation through the channel.
Herein, we report the design and the simple one-step in-situ synthesis of photo-responsive MOF sub-nanochannels (SNCs) with an efficient light-responsive ion transport property that has never been observed in conventional 1D nanochannels. The MOFSNC was fabricated by in-situ growth of zirconium based UiO-66 crystals and AZO molecules into a 1D nanochannel embedded within a polymer membrane (Figure 1a). The UiO$66^{[28]}$ channels are three-dimensional (3D) and comprised of octahedral and tetrahedral cavities $(\sim12\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ and ${\sim}9\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ , respectively) connected by ${\sim}6\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ triangular windows (Figure 1b). Inside the 3D UiO-66 channel, the guest AZO, with comparable size to the MOF cavity, would conformationally transform between cis- and transisomers under UV and heat stimuli (Figure 1b), regulating the ion flux through the channel between open and close states. An onoff ratio up to $\sim17.8$ is achieved in the UiO-66-AZO MOFSNC, which is about one order of magnitude higher than those observed in conventional 1D nanochannels. Moreover, the cyclic measurement of transmembrane ionic current proves the reversible and cyclical light-responsive switching of the nanochannel. This proof-of-concept study not only provides an approach to realize high gating performance in artificial ion channels with commercially available small responsive molecules, but also presents the possibility for more complex and multifunctional biomimetic ion channels by introducing MOFSNC as an angstrom-scaled platform.
## Results and Discussion
A single bullet-shaped nanochannel was fabricated in polyethylene terephthalate (PET, $12\upmu\mathrm{m}$ thickness) membrane by surfactant controlling and ion track-etching technology[29]. Due to the difficulty of locating the nanochannel in the single-channel membrane, a multi-channel membrane with channel density of $10^{8}\ c m^{-2}$ was fabricated with the same method for further characterization of the nanochannel morphology. The calculated mean diameter of the base side of the channel is $264\pm42$ nm, while the tip side is $39\pm7$ nm (Figure S1). The scanning electron microscope (SEM) images show the cross section of the single bullet-shaped nanochannel (Figures 2a and S1). After etching, the inner wall of the PET nanochannel is exposed with the same terminal group as the benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid (BDC) linkers, which promotes the in-situ growth of UiO-66 MOFs (Figure S2a).
The light-responsive UiO-66-AZO nanochannel was constructed by an in-situ solvothermal method, in which AZO was introduced during the crystallization of UiO-66. SEM images of the cross sections of the PET-UiO-66 nanochannel (Figures 2b and S1) show that the channel is fully filled up with UiO-66 crystals. The energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) mapping of the nanochannel before and after MOF growth, as well as the X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of PET, PET-UiO-66, PET-UiO-66-AZO nanochannels also confirmed the successful growth of UiO-66 crystals[28] inside the PET nanochannels (Figure 2c and S2). The XRD patterns also indicate that the introduction of AZO during UiO-66 crystallization would not affect the MOF structure. According to $N_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherm profiles, UiO-66- AZO crystals had a Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area of $761\pm4\mathsf{m}^{2}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , which is much lower than $,036{\pm}4\mathsf{m}^{2}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ for UiO66 crystals (Figure 2d). The pore size distributions revealed that UiO-66 had nano-sized cavities and angstrom-scaled windows of ${\sim}6\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ in diameter and the window size decreased when AZO was added (Figure 2e). Both BET surface areas and pore size distribution provided evidence for the existence of guesting AZO in UiO-66 crystals. These are consistent with the Fouriertransform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) results (Figure S3c), in which the UiO-66-AZO shows the AZO peak around $1580~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ . The more weight loss in UiO-66-AZO than UiO-66 in thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) further confirms the existence of AZO in MOF (Figure S3d), and the molar ratio of AZO molecules to the unit cells of UiO-66 in UiO-66-AZO crystals is $47.0\%$ . These results confirmed that the AZO molecules were trapped inside the UiO-66 cavities during the MOF crystallization process. Therefore, a light-responsive biomimetic MOF-based ion channels confined with molecular gates was successfully fabricated inside the PET nanochannel by a simple one-step method.
The light responsive property of the PET-UiO-66-AZO SNC system was investigated by measuring the current-voltage $(I-V)$ curves of the PET-UiO-66-AZO channel and reference PET-UiO66 channel using 0.1 M LiCl aqueous solution. The pristine bulletshaped PET-nanochannels exhibited a sharp rectified ion current (Figure S4) due to the negatively charged ionize carboxyl groups on the inner surface of the channel and the asymmetric bullet channel shape. After the incorporation of UiO-66 and UiO-66- AZO into the PET nanochannels, the I-V curve became symmetric and the transmembrane current significantly dropped at negative voltages (Figure S4). The reduction in rectification and current may also support the successful construction of UiO-66 SNC into the PET nanochannel, which would work well for further hosting the responsive AZO molecule.
For PET nanochannel and PET-UiO-66-SNC, no obvious change in $\boldsymbol{I-V}$ curves could be observed before and after UV $(365~\mathsf{n m})$ irradiation (Figures 3a and S5). Nevertheless, as seen from Figure 3b, the transmembrane ionic current of PET-UiO-66-AZO channel reduced remarkably after UV radiation, suggesting that the channel switches from open-state to close-state. The on-off ratio (Figure 3c), defined as the quotient of ionic current at openstate divided by that of the close-state, of the PET-UiO-66-AZOSNC was $17.7{\pm}0.5$ at a voltage of $+2\lor$ and $17.8{\pm}0.3\$ at $-2~\mathsf{V}$ , while both PET and PET-UiO-66 channel showed on-off ratio of $\mathord{\sim}1$ . Average on-off ratios of four reproduced PET-UiO-66-AZOSNC samples were $14.0{\pm}2.6\$ at the voltage of $+2\lor$ and $11.8{\pm}3.4\$ at -2 V (Figure S6), indicating good reproducibility of the UiO-66- AZO-SNCs. The significant current variation of the PET-UiO-66- AZO-SNC demonstrates that the channel could be regulated by UV light, and further proves the vital role of AZO in this light-gating ion transport system.
Interestingly, the light-gating behavior observed in this 3D UiO66-AZO-SNC (where the trans- and cis-AZO correspond to the open and close states of the channel, respectively) (Figure 3d) is opposite to traditional 1D nanochannel. In a 1D nanochannel[9a, 9c, 25b], where AZO is fixed on the inner surface of the nanochannel, and the open or close state of the channel directly depends on the conformation of the stimuli-responsive AZO. Therefore, the stretched trans-AZO would transform into bent cis-AZO, leading to a close-to-open switching of the channel upon UV light (Figure 3e). Whereas, in the 3D SNC, the synergistic effect of both UiO66 frameworks and guest AZO resulted in an unusual gating property (Figure 3d). In addition, the on-off ratios observed in 3D MOFSNC are much higher than those of $\sim1.3\AA\mathrm{-}1.5^{[9\mathrm{a},9\mathrm{c}]}$ observed in traditional 1D nanochannels. Generally speaking, the transfiguration between trans- and cis- of confined AZO is not thorough[30]. Although the exact cis-trans- ratio of AZO cannot be determined, by changing the duration of stimuli (Figure S7), the isomerization between trans- and cis- AZO can be maximized to a saturation state, leading to an optimized on-off ratio of the SNC.
Figure 4 shows the gating mechanism of the UiO-66-AZO-SNC. With interesting photoisomerization nature, AZO switches from trans- to cis- conformation under UV light, changing from a rodlike planar molecular shape into a bent non-planar molecular shape. Due to the intermolecular repulsion, the trans-AZO tends to lie in the center of the octahedral cavity to keep as far away as possible from the UiO-66 frameworks for lower energy states. Therefore, the rod-shaped trans-AZO would leave spaces around it for ions to transport through the MOF cavity and windows (Figure 4a), giving high ionic current (Figure 3b). The similar values of ion current and ion conductance presented by UiO-66- AZO channel and UiO-66 channel (Figure 3a and b, Table S1) also provided evidence that the introduction of trans-AZO into the MOFSNC would only slightly reduce but not block the ion transport.
Upon UV irradiation, trans-AZO isomerized into cis-AZO[30], not only changed in its molecular shape, but also the dipole moment (from 0 to 3.1D)[9a]. When a polar molecule with electric dipole is placed in an electric field, it experiences a torque, which tends to orient itself in a direction orient itself in the direction of the field [31]. During the test, under applied voltage, the unfixed cis-AZO would align along the electric field, whose dipole directing from the negative electrode to the positive electrode, resulting in the molecular orientation. Meanwhile, in the testing environment of 0.1 M LiCl (pH adjusted to 5.6), the protonated of Zr nodes would be positively charged (Figure 4c), indicated by the positive zeta potential of UiO-66 in $p H=5.6$ water $(35.5{\pm}2.8\ \mathrm{\mV})$ and LiCl solution $(17.7\pm1.9\mathsf{m V})$ due to protonation of the Zr-OH groups on the $Z\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\mathrm{r}}}_{6}$ nodes[32]. The positively charged $Z\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\mathrm{r}}}_{6}$ nodes would attract the negative $N=N$ of cis-AZO by electrostatic attraction. Resulting from both dipole orientation and interaction with $Z\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\mathrm{r}}}_{6}$ nodes, the bent-shaped cis-AZO would block the 6 Å window of UiO-66 (Figure 4b), thus the movement of the ions passing through the MOF cage would be largely blocked from all directions, leading to a low ion flux (Figure 3b). The UiO-66 crystal structure solution from the synchrotron PXRD data (Figure S8) shows that the lattice parameter and pore size of UiO-66 slightly increase after UV irradiation, indicating the interaction of AZO with UiO-66 framework and the trans-to-cis transformation of AZO. This also indicates that the repulsion force between cis-AZO and the framework is higher compared to trans-AZO, supporting the conjectured rotation of AZO inside the UiO-66 cavity.
Figure 4d shows the $1-V$ curves of the MOFSNC with high ionic current after it was heated at $60~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , which is similar to the asprepared open-state channel, indicating the channel was switched back on. This is because the cis-AZO transformed into the trans-AZO again after heating, opening the pathway in the MOF for ion transportation. Although cis-AZO can isomerize into trans-AZO under visible light too, the $1-V$ curve of the MOFSNC after visible light irradiation remained the same as the result under UV condition. This may be because the visible light of low energy is difficult to drive the conformation change of AZO for the strong interaction between positively charged $Z\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\mathrm{r}}}_{6}$ nodes and negative $N=N$ of cis-AZO, thus it cannot manipulate the MOFSNC to openstate.
To gain more insight into the reversible switching between open and close states, the photo-responsive MOFSNC was further studied through the reversibility of the ion current through the channel. Figure 5a and b displays the on-off cycling of the AZOmodified MOFSNC between UV response and heating recovery, in which the ionic current of the MOFSNC at $+2\vee$ and $\boldsymbol{\mathbf{\mathit{\Sigma}}}\boldsymbol{\mathbf{\mathit{\Sigma}}}\boldsymbol{\mathbf{\mathit{\Sigma}}}\boldsymbol{\mathbf{\mathit{\Sigma}}}\boldsymbol{\mathbf{\mathit{\Sigma}}}\boldsymbol{\mathbf{\mathit{\Sigma}}}-\boldsymbol{2}\mathrm{\Sigma}\boldsymbol{\mathbf{\mathit{V}}}$ were cyclically switched between high and low states. After at least four cycles of switching, there is no obvious change in the ion flux of each gating state observed. As the gating property of the channel relies on the change between cis- and trans- AZO isomer, the reversible conformational switching of responsive AZOs inside the MOF cavities will lead to the reversibility of the whole PET
UiO-66-AZO system. The unchanged XRD patterns, as well as the similar morphology showed in SEM images of MOF crystals, before and after the cyclic testing further proved the stability of UiO-66-AZO under UV irradiation and heating (Figures 5c and S8). The above results confirmed the reversible and cyclic switching performance of the light-responsive PET-UiO-66-AZO nanochannel.
## Conclusion
In summary, we have demonstrated a bioinspired light-responsive MOFSNC constructed by integrating AZO molecules into 3D UiO66 frameworks confined within single polymer nanochannels. Upon UV irradiation, the guest trans-AZO molecule inside the Zrbased UiO-66 cavity can transform into cis-AZO, leading to an unusual open-to-close switching of the channel. The AZOmodified MOFSNC can be reversibly switched back to open state by thermal treatment. The stability of the nanochannel has been proved by cyclic switching between open and close states. By fabricating MOFSNCs based on the nanochannels, this research provides a new strategy for further constructing other stimuligating bioinspired ion channels based on MOFs and small responsive molecules, which can be applied in areas such as light-responsive sensors, on-demand delivery, and energy conversion systems.
