## Entry for the Table of Contents
Light-switchable metal-organic-framework (MOF) ion channels are constructed by in-situ growth of three-dimensional UiO-66 frameworks with guesting light-responsive azobenzene (AZO) into polymer nanochannels. The AZO molecules confined within MOF cavities function as molecular gates to efficiently control ion flux through the MOF channels, showing a high on-off ratio of $\mathord{\sim}17.8$ . Such a gating efficiency observed in MOF channels is one order of magnitude higher than those of conventional AZO-modified nanochannels.
